Y = X \\times_S T$", "MATH_0003": "$$\n\\xymatrix{\nX_\\etale \\ar[d]_{f_{small}} & Y_\\etale \\ar[d]^{e_{small}} \\ar[l]^{h_{small}} \\\\\nS_\\etale & T_\\etale \\ar[l]_{g_{small}}\n}\n$$", "MATH_0004": "$$\nf^{-1}g_*\\mathcal{F}\n\\longrightarrow\nh_*e^{-1}\\mathcal{F}\n$$", "MATH_0005": "$\\mathcal{F}$", "MATH_0006": "$T_\\etale$", "MATH_0007": "$\\mathcal{F}$", "MATH_0008": "$T_\\etale$", "MATH_0009": "$$\nf^{-1}Rg_*\\mathcal{F}\n\\longrightarrow\nRh_*e^{-1}\\mathcal{F}\n$$", "MATH_0010": "$K$", "MATH_0011": "$D(T_\\etale)$", "MATH_0012": "$$\nf^{-1}Rg_*K\n\\longrightarrow\nRh_*e^{-1}K\n$$", "REF_0001": "\\ref{sites-section-pullback}", "REF_0002": "\\ref{sites-cohomology-lemma-base-change-map-flat-case}", "REF_0003": "\\ref{sites-cohomology-remark-base-change}", "STRUCT_0001": "\\noindent"}, "render": {"prefix": "", "suffix": ""}, "risk_level": "R3", "schema_version": 2, "source_commit": "a04446e57ec1fbc252a871afcec7752fb2807b14", "source_math_hash": "sha256:ebaa1aa3573b99714007a5c6fdff267efc4cd5c9a7c33545bb502e924f9c13e5", "source_math_skeleton_hash": "sha256:7eff9e75b22f990f48536b77f2e442656f54b5384da669d1c01914a16199b0e1", "source_status": "CURRENT", "source_structure_hash": "sha256:15282e2e87b194045ee7c730c33ebdcccaf209232a4535b74403f28c67b54198", "source_text": "\n\n<STRUCT_0001>\nIf you are interested in either the smooth base change theorem\nor the proper base change theorem, you should skip directly to\nthe corresponding sections.\nIn this section and the next few sections we consider commutative diagrams\n<MATH_0001>\nof schemes; we usually assume this diagram is cartesian,\ni.e., <MATH_0002>. A commutative diagram as above\ngives rise to a commutative diagram\n<MATH_0003>\nof small <ACCENT_0001>tale sites. Let us use the notation\n<MATHSEG_0001> and<MATHSEG_0002>\nBy Sites, Section <REF_0001>\nwe get a base change or pullback map\n<MATH_0004>\nfor a sheaf <MATH_0005> on <MATH_0006>. If <MATH_0007> is an abelian\nsheaf on <MATH_0008>, then we get a derived base change map\n<MATH_0009>\nsee Cohomology on Sites, Lemma\n<REF_0002>.\nFinally, if <MATH_0010> is an arbitrary object of <MATH_0011>\nthere is a base change map\n<MATH_0012>\nsee\nCohomology on Sites, Remark <REF_0003>.\n\n", "source_text_hash": "sha256:9fa78e12ba29f97178c9085ae29b42454b2e235e8cb07542cdbbf4ca36956b00", "unit_id": "tag:0EZQ:prose-0001"}
